<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
<p style="text-align: center;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1 of 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> International English</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How English Is Used in Workplaces Globally</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the linear structure of a short formal talk. Interactive: Facilitate discussions on future career paths.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow key points in a structured talk about career planning (e.g., Explain the speaker's advice on gaining experience, for example, "He said that we should gain more experience in our field by taking on diverse projects or internships.")
            Can facilitate discussions on career options, prompting classmates to share ideas on communication-related skills (e.g., "Which industries in Panama require English fluency, and why?")
            Can ask follow-up questions to encourage classmates to elaborate on career choices (e.g., "Can you explain which skills can help you advance your career?")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images showing people from diverse backgrounds collaborating in various professional settings (e.g., a virtual meeting, a construction site with international workers, a medical conference). Students discuss in pairs: "What language do you think these people are using to communicate?" and "Why is English important in these global interactions?" (2 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary words from the curriculum list relevant to the listening task, writing them on the board: *global, international, communication, skills, fluency, workplaces, cross-cultural, business, resources, access*. The teacher models the pronunciation, specifically highlighting the long /e/ sound in words like 'relevant' and 'necessary' (if applicable from the list, otherwise new examples) and the alternation of full and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words like 'communication' /kəˌmjuː.nɪˈkeɪ.ʃən/ and 'accessible' /əkˈses.ə.bəl/. Students repeat chorally and individually. (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is 'global,' does it only happen in one country or worldwide?" (Worldwide)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you have 'fluency' in a language, do you speak it slowly or smoothly and easily?" (Smoothly and easily)</li>
                    </ul>
                    Teacher provides examples for each word in context, e.g., "Having *fluency* in English can open many doors in *global* *workplaces*." (3 min)
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "International English".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of a short formal talk (approx. 2-3 minutes) about the importance of English and communication skills in global career planning. The talk includes examples of the present perfect (e.g., "English has become an international language..."), modals for necessity (e.g., "You must learn to adapt... It could be a good idea to gain experience..."), and several target vocabulary words from the warm-up.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interprets main ideas and specific details from spoken texts in formal contexts related to professional development.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listening (Gist):</b> Students listen once. Teacher asks: "What is the main topic of this talk?" and "What is one key message the speaker wants us to remember about our future careers?" (2 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listening (Detailed):</b> Students listen again. Teacher provides 3-4 True/False statements related to specific advice or facts presented in the talk (e.g., "The speaker mentioned that gaining experience through internships is vital." True/False). Students mark their answers. (4 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Follow-up:</b> Students compare their True/False answers with a partner, discussing any discrepancies. (2 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening for specific grammatical structures (Present Perfect, Modals) and target vocabulary in context, reinforcing receptive accuracy.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill Listening):</b> The teacher distributes a handout with a partial transcript of the formal talk, containing blanks where the target vocabulary (e.g., *fluency, cross-cultural, business, enhance*) or present perfect verbs (e.g., "has become") should be. Students listen to the audio for a third time and fill in the blanks. (4 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Modal Identification):</b> Students re-listen to specific segments of the talk identified by the teacher. They are tasked with identifying sentences containing modals (must, could, should, can) and discussing what necessity, possibility, or advice these modals express. For instance, "He said that we *should* gain more experience..." (advice). (4 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students compare their completed gap-fills and identified modal functions. They take turns reading aloud the completed sentences, paying attention to the pronunciation of multisyllabic words and the /e/ sound. The teacher circulates, offering assistance and correction as needed. (3 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">11 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "International English".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Discussion on Future Career Paths.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form groups of three or four. The teacher gives them discussion prompts based on the formal talk and the theme.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Prompt 1:</b> "Based on the talk, how has English become an international language, and what impact does it have on global business today?" (Using present perfect).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Prompt 2:</b> "Thinking about your future careers, which industries in Panama do you believe require English fluency, and why? What skills could help you advance?" (Using modals).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Prompt 3:</b> Students take turns facilitating the discussion within their group. One student asks an indirect question to prompt classmates (e.g., "Can you explain which skills can help you advance your career?"), while others respond and ask follow-up questions to encourage elaboration.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on interactive listening, facilitating discussion, using target vocabulary (e.g., *workplaces, communication, cross-cultural*), present perfect, modals, and indirect questions in a formal, career-focused context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher observes, providing minimal intervention to encourage natural communication and fluency.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment during group discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate actively in their small group discussions, demonstrating their ability to follow key points from the formal talk, facilitate discussions, and ask follow-up questions. Each student also assesses one peer.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Understood key points of the formal talk (evident in contributions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used target vocabulary (e.g., *global, fluency, cross-cultural communication*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used present perfect and modals correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asked clear indirect and follow-up questions to facilitate discussion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of /e/ sound and multisyllabic words was generally intelligible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Actively listened to and responded to classmates' ideas.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing you learned today about English in global workplaces from the audio?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one communication skill you *must* or *should* develop for your future career?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (Homework/Journal):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did I follow the main points and details in the formal talk today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What challenges did I face when trying to facilitate the discussion or ask follow-up questions, and what can I do next time to improve my interactive listening?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in terms of engaging students with the listening material and prompting interaction? (e.g., The variety of listening tasks kept students focused.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson, especially regarding pronunciation or specific grammar points? (e.g., Some students struggled with the pace of the formal talk; perhaps pre-teaching more complex vocabulary or providing a glossary for the audio text would be beneficial for Lesson 2's reading text.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core vocabulary and concepts through listening. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will delve deeper into the same theme and vocabulary by reading an article related to English in global workplaces, providing further contextualization and reinforcement of the introduced terms.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">4 minutes</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>